const MN=["First Monthly Training","Second Monthly Assessment","Third Monthly Assessment"];
const addM=(m,R)=>R.forEach(r=>Q.push([r[0],r[1],r[2],r[3],r[4],r[5],r[6],"📅 "+MN[m-1],r[7]||"",m]));
addM(1,[
[0,"Complete",0,"Complete with what is scientifically appropriate: Putting living organisms with similar characteristics in groups is called ______",["Classification"],"Scientists sort living things into groups by what is alike, like plants and animals. This is classification.","At home we sort clothes: shirts in one drawer, socks in another."],
[1,"Complete",0,"Complete: The active stage that hatches from the butterfly’s egg and feeds voraciously on leaves is called the ______ stage.",["larva"],"The larva (caterpillar) comes out of the egg and eats leaves all the time to grow.","A hungry caterpillar eats holes in leaves.","bf"],
[0,"Complete",0,"Complete: Fennec fox feet are characterized by the presence of ______",["thick fur"],"Thick fur on the soles protects the fox’s feet from the hot sand.","Sandals protect your feet on a hot beach.","fox"],
[0,"Complete",0,"Complete: Most desert animals are active ______",["at night"],"Desert days are very hot, so most animals rest in cool burrows and come out at night.","In summer we go out in the evening, when it is cooler.","hab"],
[0,"Complete",1,"Complete: The Luff plant lives in an environment with ______ water and ______ soil.",["plenty of water and moist soil"],"The luff plant needs a lot of water, so it grows where water is plenty and the soil is moist.","Gardens near the Nile stay green because the soil is always wet."],
[2,"Give Reason",1,"Give reason: The seed does not need sunlight in the initial stages of germination (when it is below the soil surface).",["It uses the food stored in its cotyledons, so it doesn’t need light yet."],"Under the soil there is no light. The seed lives on the food stored inside it until its first leaves reach the light.","Like a packed lunch on a trip: you eat it until you can find food."],
[0,"Give Reason",0,"Give reason: The importance of using a magnifying glass while examining the surrounding environment.",["To see small details clearly (it makes things look bigger)."],"A magnifying glass makes small things look bigger, so we see tiny parts like the veins of a leaf.","Grandparents use a magnifying glass to read small letters."],
[0,"Give Reason",1,"Give reason: Camels drink a large quantity of water at once.",["Water is scarce in the desert, so it drinks a lot at once and keeps it in its body for a long time."],"In the desert water is hard to find. A camel drinks a huge amount at once, then can live many days without drinking. (Its hump stores fat, not water.)","Before a long trip with no shops, you fill a big water bottle."],
[0,"Short Answer",1,"Complete the following table (Part of the living organism – The Importance): The fennec fox’s large ears / The jerboa’s hind legs",["Fennec fox’s large ears: lose body heat (keep it cool). Jerboa’s hind legs: jump far to escape enemies."],"Each part is an adaptation: big ears let heat leave the body, and long hind legs help the jerboa leap away fast.","Kangaroos also jump on long back legs.","fox"],
[2,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: The part that anchors the plant in the soil is called the bud.",["The part that anchors the plant in the soil is called the root."],"Roots grow down into the soil and hold the plant firmly. A bud is a young flower or leaf.","A tent peg holds the tent in the ground, like roots hold a plant.","pl"],
[2,"Correct",1,"Correct the underlined word: The corn plant seed is large and has white and black stripes.",["The sunflower plant seed is large and has white and black stripes."],"Sunflower seeds have black and white stripes. Corn seeds are yellow.","The striped seeds sold as a snack (lebb) are sunflower seeds."],
[1,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: The bee’s body consists of 4 parts.",["The bee’s body consists of 3 parts."],"Every insect body has 3 parts: head, thorax and abdomen.","An ant has 3 body parts too."],
[0,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: The jerboa has thick fur on the soles of its feet.",["The Fennec fox has thick fur on the soles of its feet."],"The fennec fox walks on hot sand, so fur on its soles protects its feet. The jerboa is known for its long hind legs.","Fur on the fox’s feet works like slippers.","fox"],
[0,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: The camel lives in the Nile River environment",["The frog (or Egyptian goose) lives in the Nile River environment."],"The camel is adapted to the dry desert. The frog needs water and moist soil, like the Nile banks.","You see frogs near canals and ponds, and camels in the desert."],
[2,"What Happens?",1,"What happens if a plant seed is placed in the soil and provided with water?",["It germinates (if it also has air and suitable warmth)."],"Water makes the seed swell and split, then a small root and shoot come out. This is germination.","Put beans on wet cotton: after a few days they sprout."],
[1,"What Happens?",2,"What happens if the larval stage is absent from the butterfly’s life cycle?",["There would be no feeding and growth, so no pupa or adult."],"The larva is the stage that eats and grows. Without it there is no stored food for the pupa, so no butterfly.","A building with no ground floor cannot have upper floors.","bf"],
[0,"What Happens?",1,"What happens if the ears of a Fennec fox were small in size?",["It can’t lose body heat well, so it suffers in the desert heat."],"Big ears let heat leave the fox’s body. Small ears would keep the heat in.","A small fan cools a room less than a big one.","fox"],
[0,"What Happens?",1,"What happens if we moved a frog that lives in a river to the desert?",["It can’t find water and moist soil, so it dies."],"The frog is adapted to wet places. The desert is dry, so it cannot get its needs.","A house plant left in the hot sun with no water dries out."],
[1,"Choose",0,"Choose the correct answer: Which of the following represents the correct order of the stages in a butterfly life cycle",["Egg → Larva → Pupa → Adult butterfly","Egg → Pupa → Larva → Adult butterfly","Larva → Egg → Pupa → Adult butterfly","Pupa → Larva → Egg → Adult butterfly"],"The egg hatches into a larva, the larva becomes a pupa, and the pupa becomes an adult butterfly.","A story: tiny egg, hungry caterpillar, quiet cocoon, then a butterfly.","bf"],
[2,"Compare",1,"Compare between the stem of plant and the root of plant (In terms of the main function of each).",["Root: anchors the plant in the soil and absorbs water and salts. Stem: supports the plant and carries water to the leaves."],"The root works under the ground; the stem works above it, like a pole and a pipe.","Roots are like a tent’s pegs; the stem is like the tent pole.","pl"],
[0,"Give Reason",1,"Explain: Some desert plants have small-sized leaves.",["To reduce water loss in the hot, dry desert."],"Leaves lose water into the air. Small leaves lose less water, which helps in the dry desert.","A small bottle opening loses less water than a wide open bowl."],
[2,"Short Answer",1,"What is the better time to plant bean and sunflower?",["Bean: autumn/winter (cool months). Sunflower: spring/summer (warm months)."],"Each plant likes its own weather: fava beans like cool months, sunflowers like warm, sunny months.","Sunflowers turn toward the bright summer Sun."],
[2,"Complete",0,"Complete with the correct number: The number of leaves that emerge directly upon the germination of a fava bean seed is ______",["Two"],"The fava bean seed has two seed leaves (cotyledons), so two leaves appear first.","Split a soaked fava bean and you see two halves."],
[1,"Complete",0,"Complete with the correct number: The number of a butterfly’s legs is ______",["6"],"A butterfly is an insect, and every insect has 6 legs.","Count: 3 legs on each side make 6."],
[1,"Complete",0,"Complete with the correct number: The number of stages in a bee’s life cycle is ______",["4"],"Egg, larva, pupa and adult: 4 stages, like the butterfly.","Like 4 steps on a staircase."],
[1,"Define",0,"Define: The larva.",["The stage that hatches from the egg and eats leaves a lot to grow."],"The larva is the baby insect that comes out of the egg. Its job is to eat and grow.","The caterpillar is the larva of the butterfly.","bf"],
[0,"Define",0,"Define: The location",["The place where the organism lives and finds its needs."],"This is the organism’s home (habitat), where it finds food, water and shelter.","Your home is where you find food, water and a bed."],
[2,"Define",1,"Define: The cotyledons (seed leaves)",["Two seed leaves that store food for the young plant."],"Cotyledons are inside the seed. They feed the young plant until its green leaves can make food.","Like a lunch box packed for the young plant."],
[0,"Short Answer",1,"What is the importance of: Adaptation of living organisms.",["It helps living organisms survive in their habitat."],"Adaptations are special body parts or behaviors that help living things live where they are.","A camel’s wide feet help it walk on sand without sinking."],
[1,"Short Answer",1,"What is the importance of: Molten process for larva.",["It lets the larva shed its tight skin and grow bigger."],"The larva’s skin does not stretch, so it sheds it to grow.","You change to bigger clothes when you grow."]]);
addM(2,[
[3,"Complete",0,"Complete: An object that emits its own light, such as the Sun and lamps, is called a ______",["light source"],"A light source makes its own light. The Moon is not one; it only reflects sunlight.","A candle and a torch are light sources too."],
[3,"Complete",1,"Complete: Shiny and smooth surfaces reflect light regularly in ______",["one direction"],"Smooth, shiny surfaces send light back neatly in one direction, so we see clear images.","A calm lake shows the trees like a mirror."],
[3,"Complete",0,"Complete: Sunlight travels in a ______ line unless an object obstructs its path.",["straight"],"Light goes straight until something blocks it.","Sunbeams through a window look like straight lines.","li"],
[3,"Complete",1,"Complete: When sunlight is focused on to a single point using a magnifying lens, the space becomes more ______ and its ______ rises.",["bright; temperature"],"All the light is collected in a small spot, so it is brighter and hotter.","The hot spot can even burn paper.","le"],
[3,"Complete",0,"Complete: Shadows are always formed in the direction ______ of the light source.",["opposite"],"The object blocks the light, so the dark shadow is on the other side.","With the Sun in front of you, your shadow is behind you.","sh"],
[3,"Complete",1,"Complete: Sun, an object, and its shadow are always on the same ______",["line"],"Light travels in straight lines, so the Sun, the object and the shadow line up.","Sun → you → your shadow, all in one line.","sh"],
[3,"Complete",0,"Complete: A ______ is used to tell time based on the regular movement of shadows.",["sundial"],"The Sun moves across the sky the same way every day, so a stick’s shadow can show the time.","A clock with no batteries, moved by the Sun."],
[3,"Complete",1,"Complete: The ground in sunny places is more ______, while in shady places it is cooler and more ______",["hot; humid"],"Sunlight heats the ground and dries it. Shade keeps the ground cool and damp.","Sand in the sun burns your feet; sand under a tree is cool and a little damp."],
[4,"Complete",1,"Complete: When an object vibrates, it ______, and when this vibration stops ______",["makes sound; the sound stops"],"Sound is made by vibration. No vibration means no sound.","Touch a ringing bell and the ringing stops."],
[4,"Complete",1,"Complete: When the magnitude of an object’s vibration increases, ______ of the sound increases, and the sound becomes ______",["volume; louder"],"Bigger vibrations make a louder sound.","Hitting a drum hard is louder than tapping it."],
[3,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: Rough surfaces reflect light regularly in one direction.",["Smooth (shiny) surfaces reflect light regularly in one direction."],"Rough surfaces scatter light in many directions; smooth ones reflect it in one direction.","You see your face in a mirror, not in a wall."],
[3,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: Light reflected from a mirror travels in a curved line.",["Light reflected from a mirror travels in a straight line."],"Light always travels in straight lines, even after bouncing off a mirror.","A torch beam on a mirror bounces off in a straight line."],
[3,"Correct",1,"Correct the underlined word: The larger the area of the light focused by a magnifying lens, the higher its temperature rises.",["The smaller the area of the light focused by a magnifying lens, the higher its temperature rises."],"The smaller the spot, the more the light is packed together, so it gets hotter.","Sun on your whole hand feels warm; the same light in a tiny dot feels very hot.","le"],
[3,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: A shadow forms in the same direction as the light source.",["A shadow forms in the opposite direction of the light source."],"The shadow is always on the side away from the light.","A lamp on your left puts your shadow on your right.","sh"],
[3,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined words: The shadows are very long at midday at noon when Sun is high in the sky.",["The shadows are very short at midday (noon) when the Sun is high in the sky."],"When the Sun is above us, the shadow is short and right under the object.","At 12 o’clock your shadow is a small spot under your feet."],
[3,"Correct",1,"Correct the underlined words: White paper heats up faster than black paper when sunlight is focused on it.",["White paper heats up slower than black paper when sunlight is focused on it."],"Black takes in more light and heat; white reflects much of it.","A black car seat in summer is hotter than a white one."],
[4,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: When vibrations stop, the sound continues.",["When vibrations stop, the sound stops."],"Sound needs vibration. When the vibration stops, the sound stops.","When a fan is turned off, its hum stops."],
[4,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: Plucking a rubber band gently produces a loud sound with large vibrations.",["Plucking a rubber band strongly produces a loud sound with large vibrations."],"A strong pluck makes big vibrations and a loud sound; a gentle pluck is quiet.","A strong pluck of an oud string is louder."],
[4,"Correct",0,"Correct the underlined word: Sound travels better through a string telephone when the string is slack.",["Sound travels better through a string telephone when the string is taut (tight)."],"A tight string carries the vibrations from cup to cup.","A tight guitar string vibrates well.","s1"],
[3,"Give Reason",1,"Give reason: Black paper burns when sunlight is focused on it using a magnifying lens.",["Black absorbs more heat from the focused light."],"Dark colours take in light and turn it into heat.","A black T-shirt in the sun feels hotter than a white one.","le"],
[3,"Give Reason",1,"Give reason: A stick’s shadow is short at midday and long in the morning and evening.",["The Sun is high at midday, and low in the morning and evening."],"High Sun gives a short shadow. Low Sun gives a long shadow.","Your shadow looks very tall at sunset."],
[3,"Give Reason",1,"Give reason: The ground in sunny places is warmer than ground in shady places.",["They receive direct sunlight and absorb its heat."],"The Sun heats the ground it shines on. Shade blocks the sunlight.","Sand in the sun burns your feet; sand in the shade is cool."],
[4,"Give Reason",1,"Give reason: Hearing the sound of musical instruments when they are plucked or struck.",["Plucking or striking makes them vibrate, which makes sound."],"Plucking a string or hitting a drum makes it vibrate, and vibration makes sound.","Hit a darbuka: its skin vibrates and you hear the beat."],
[4,"Give Reason",1,"Give reason: The sound instantly cut off in a string telephone if the string is grabbed in the middle.",["The string stops vibrating."],"Your hand stops the vibrations travelling along the string, so the sound cannot reach the other cup.","Touch a ringing bell and the ringing stops.","s3"],
[3,"What Happens?",1,"What happens when Sun moves from East to West (relative to: the direction of the shadow)?",["The shadow moves from West to East."],"The shadow is always opposite the Sun. As the Sun goes West, the shadow turns East.","A tree’s shadow points West in the morning and East in the evening.","sh"],
[3,"What Happens?",1,"What happens when a shady place becomes sunny (relative to the temperature and humidity of the ground)?",["Temperature rises; humidity decreases."],"Sunlight warms the ground and dries it.","Wet clothes dry quickly in the sun."],
[3,"What Happens?",1,"What happens when a small amount to powder or water spray is scattered in the area where reflected light passes through?",["The path of the light can be seen."],"Tiny bits in the air shine when light hits them, so we can see the beam.","In a dusty room you can see sunbeams.","li"],
[4,"What Happens?",1,"What happens when an object begins to vibrate and make a sound (relative to the air surrounding the object)?",["The surrounding air vibrates too."],"The vibrating object shakes the air next to it, and the air carries the sound.","Stand near a big speaker: you can feel the air shake."],
[4,"What Happens?",1,"What happens when hitting a drum or a musical triangle strongly (relative to the amount of vibration and the intensity of the sound)?",["More vibration and a louder sound."],"A strong hit makes bigger vibrations, so the sound is louder.","Clapping hard is louder than clapping softly."],
[4,"What Happens?",1,"What happens when the string is stretched tightly between the two cups in a string telephone (relative to the sound intensity in telephone)?",["The sound is clear and strong."],"A tight string carries vibrations well.","A tight drum skin makes a clear sound.","s1"],
[3,"Short Answer",2,"Mention three safety precautions that must be followed when conducting sunlight-focusing experiments using a magnifying lens.",["Don’t look at the Sun or the light spot; use a heat-proof surface; keep water nearby, and work with an adult."],"The focused spot is very hot and bright: it can hurt your eyes and start a fire.","When cooking, an adult stays near the stove. With a lens, an adult stays near too.","le"],
[3,"Short Answer",2,"Explain how shadows are formed.",["An opaque object blocks light travelling in straight lines, so a dark area forms behind it."],"Light cannot bend around the object, so the place behind it gets no light. That is the shadow.","Your hand in front of a torch makes a hand shadow on the wall.","sh"],
[3,"Short Answer",2,"Discuss the working idea of a sundial and how shadow movement is used to determine approximate time.",["A stick’s shadow moves as the Sun moves across the sky; its position and length on the marks show the approximate time."],"The Sun moves the same way every day, so the shadow reaches each mark at about the same time each day.","Like a clock hand that the Sun moves for us."],
[3,"Compare",2,"Compare sunny places and shaded places in terms of: temperature, humidity level",["Temperature: sunny places higher (warmer), shaded places lower (cooler). Humidity: sunny places lower (drier), shaded places higher (more humid)."],"Sunlight heats and dries; shade keeps things cool and damp.","Under a tree the ground stays cool and moist."],
[4,"Short Answer",2,"How does sound travel from its source to the human’s ear? Explain, that showing the role of air vibration.",["The vibrating source makes the air around it vibrate; the air vibrations travel to the ear, so we hear the sound."],"Sound passes from the source to the air, then through the air to our ears.","Like a line of people: each one gently pushes the next."],
[4,"Short Answer",2,"Based on how a simple string telephone is made, mention only two points that make the sound reaching your ear is more clear.",["Keep the string tight (taut), and don’t touch or hold the string. (Use a long, straight string between the cups.)"],"A tight, free string carries the vibrations best.","A tight guitar string gives a clear sound.","so"]]);
addM(3,[
[5,"Complete",0,"Complete: Force is an effect that causes a stationary object to move, stops a moving object, or changes its ______",["direction"],"A force is a push or a pull. It can start, stop or turn something.","Kicking a moving ball to the side changes its direction."],
[5,"Complete",0,"Complete: The greater the force, the ______ its effect on the object.",["greater"],"A bigger push or pull has a bigger effect.","A hard kick sends the ball farther than a soft one."],
[5,"Complete",0,"Complete: When strong winds blow on a wind-powered car, it travels a ______ distance compared to weak winds.",["longer"],"Strong wind gives a bigger force, so the car goes farther.","A strong wind blows leaves far away.","sa"],
[5,"Complete",1,"Complete: As the surface area of the sail that catches the wind increases, ______ the force exerted by the wind on it (given a constant wind force).",["the greater"],"A bigger sail catches more air, so the wind pushes it harder.","An open umbrella on a windy day pulls you more than a closed one.","sa"],
[6,"Complete",0,"Complete: A rubber band possesses a property that allows it to return to its ______ after being stretched.",["original shape"],"Rubber is elastic: after stretching, it goes back to its first shape.","A hair tie goes back to its size after you stretch it.","ru"],
[6,"Complete",1,"Complete: When the number of rubber bands increases, the forces resulting from each band returning to its original shape are ______",["greater"],"Each band adds its own push, so more bands give a bigger force.","Four friends pulling a rope are stronger than one."],
[7,"Complete",0,"Complete: The rate of water leakage into the soil depends on the ______ that make up the soil.",["particle size"],"Big particles leave big gaps for water; tiny particles leave tiny gaps.","A strainer with big holes lets water out faster.","sl"],
[7,"Complete",0,"Complete: Water leaks slowly in ______ soil because its particles are fine and tightly packed.",["clay"],"Clay has very fine, tightly packed particles, so water moves slowly.","After rain, muddy ground keeps puddles.","sl"],
[7,"Complete",1,"Complete: Ancient Egyptians sprayed ______ on the sand in front of the sledge to make the surface slipperier when moving statues.",["water"],"Wet sand is firm and smooth, so the heavy sledge slides more easily.","Wet sand is firm enough to build a sandcastle; dry sand falls apart."],
[5,"Correct",0,"Correct the statement: The smaller the force acting on an object, the greater its effect.",["The greater the force acting on an object, the greater its effect."],"A bigger force has a bigger effect.","A hard push moves a swing higher."],
[5,"Correct",1,"Correct the statement: Reducing the surface area of the sail increases the force produced by the wind.",["Increasing the surface area of the sail increases the force produced by the wind."],"A bigger sail catches more wind, so the force is bigger.","Big feluccas on the Nile have large sails.","sa"],
[5,"Correct",1,"Correct the statement: The distance traveled by a wind-powered car is constant and does not change with the change of wind strength.",["The distance changes: the stronger the wind, the longer the distance."],"More wind force pushes the car farther.","A paper flies farther when the fan is on high."],
[6,"Correct",0,"Correct the statement: A rubber band produces force when it is slack and non - stretched.",["A rubber band produces force when it is stretched."],"A band only pushes when it is stretched and then goes back to its shape.","A slingshot only works when you pull it."],
[6,"Correct",0,"Correct the statement: Increasing the number of rubber bands decreases the distance traveled by the car.",["Increasing the number of rubber bands increases the distance traveled by the car."],"More bands give more force, so the car goes farther.","Two people pushing a cart move it farther than one.","ru"],
[7,"Correct",0,"Correct the statement: Sandy soil is characterized by having very small spaces between its particles.",["Sandy soil has very large spaces between its particles."],"Sand grains are big, so the gaps between them are big.","Marbles in a jar leave big gaps.","sl"],
[7,"Correct",1,"Correct the statement: Water leak through Nile River silt faster than through desert sand.",["Water leaks through desert sand faster than through Nile River silt."],"Sand has big gaps (fast); silt has tiny gaps (slow).","Water disappears fast on the beach but makes puddles on mud.","sl"],
[6,"Correct",0,"Correct the statement: Using 3 rubber bands provides a weaker moving force for the car than using a single band.",["Using 3 rubber bands provides a stronger moving force for the car than using a single band."],"Three bands push together, so the force is bigger.","Three strings together make a stronger rope."],
[5,"Give Reason",1,"Give the scientific reason: A wind-powered car travel a longer distance when the wind is strong.",["Strong wind gives a greater force on the sail."],"The stronger the wind, the bigger the push on the sail.","On a windy day it is hard to walk against the wind.","sa"],
[5,"Give Reason",1,"Give the scientific reason: A sailboat with a large sail move a longer distance than a boat with a small sail when exposed to the same wind.",["A large sail catches more wind, so the force is greater."],"More sail area catches more air, so the force is bigger.","A big kite pulls harder than a small kite.","sa"],
[6,"Give Reason",1,"Give the scientific reason: The car rush forward when a stretched rubber band is released",["The band returns to its original shape and pushes the car."],"The stretched band stores force; when released it snaps back and pushes.","Let go of a stretched hair tie and it shoots forward.","ru"],
[6,"Give Reason",1,"Give the scientific reason: The distance traveled by a car increase when using two rubber bands instead of one stretched to the same length.",["Two bands give a greater pushing force than one band."],"Two bands add their forces together.","Two friends pushing a swing make it go higher."],
[7,"Give Reason",1,"Give the scientific reason: Water leaks quickly in sandy soil.",["Its particles are large with large spaces between them."],"Water slips through the big gaps.","Water goes through a big hole faster than a tiny one.","sl"],
[7,"Give Reason",1,"Give the scientific reason: Nile River silt retains water for a long time and exhibits slow water leakage.",["Its particles are very fine with tiny spaces between them."],"Tiny gaps make water move slowly and stay longer.","A sponge with tiny holes holds water."],
[7,"Give Reason",1,"Give the scientific reason: Nile River silt is suitable for agriculture.",["It keeps water and is rich in nutrients."],"Plants need water and food. Silt keeps both near the roots.","Fields along the Nile stay green and full of crops."],
[7,"Give Reason",2,"Give the scientific reason: Ancient Egyptians spray water on the sand in front of the sledge while transporting huge statues?",["Wet sand is firmer and smoother, so the sledge slides more easily with less force (less friction)."],"Dry sand piles up in front of the sledge; wet sand stays firm and flat.","Wet sand on the beach is easy to walk on; dry sand is hard."],
[5,"What Happens?",1,"What happens when a fan speed is increased from low to high, to the distance traveled by a wind-powered car?",["The car travels a longer distance."],"Higher fan speed makes stronger wind, a bigger force and a longer distance.","A paper flies farther with the fan on high.","sa"],
[5,"What Happens?",2,"What happens to the force acting on the car if we remove the sail Completely while it is exposed to wind?",["The force becomes very small (almost none); the car barely moves."],"Without a sail, there is almost nothing to catch the wind.","The wind pulls an open umbrella, but not a closed one."],
[6,"What Happens?",1,"What happens to the distance traveled by a rubber-band-powered car when the stretch length of the rubber band is increased from 5 cm to 15 cm?",["The distance increases (the car travels farther)."],"More stretch gives more force, so the car goes farther.","A toy car you pull back more runs farther.","ru"],
[6,"What Happens?",1,"What happens to the force produced by the rubber band after it fully returns to its original shape?",["No force (zero)."],"When the band is back to its normal shape it is not stretched, so it gives no force.","A hair tie lying on the table pushes nothing."],
[6,"What Happens?",1,"What happens to the force that affecting on the car when using 3 rubber bands together instead of one band at the same stretch length?",["The force increases (becomes greater)."],"Three bands add their forces.","Three friends pulling a rope are stronger than one."],
[7,"What Happens?",0,"What happens when a quantity of water is poured on to soil with large particles (such as desert sand)?",["The water drains (leaks) quickly."],"Big gaps let water pass fast.","Water poured on beach sand disappears quickly.","sl"],
[7,"What Happens?",0,"What happens when a quantity of water is poured on to fine-grained clay soil (such as Nile silt)?",["The water drains slowly and stays in the soil."],"Tiny gaps slow the water down.","Puddles stay on muddy ground after rain.","sl"],
[7,"What Happens?",1,"What happens to the force required to move a heavy object if the surface beneath it is made slipperier?",["Less force is needed."],"A slippery surface lets heavy things slide easily.","A box slides more easily on smooth tiles than on a carpet."],
[7,"Compare",2,"Compare sandy soil and clay soil in terms of: particle size, spaces between particles, and water leakage speed.",["Sandy soil: large particles, large spaces, fast leakage. Clay soil: very small (fine) particles, very small spaces, slow leakage."],"Big grains leave big gaps, so water is fast. Tiny grains leave tiny gaps, so water is slow.","Sand is like marbles; clay is like flour.","sl"]]);
